/* Which model a legacy inline subagent becomes, as the real runtime decides.
 *
 * Captured 2026-10-16 against the legacy-child-boundary fixed installation
 * pair — `unchain==0.2.0`, wheel SHA-256
 * ea7ee78194bbf715ca8200e5a4b8ef494e7dc1fcf5a8c48bb75d4e47789984b0, sidecar
 * SHA-256 7b53b048a05011060f428aae81db8aa4ecaf7e23df0a5ab94d5d7aff3e28f5b5,
 * runtime manifest
 * sha256:f6daf3894dd947723ee7dc60392277896b88c4890d35373b26442a1833a28101,
 * CPython 3.12.10 — by asking the real store. Every row is one
 * `SkeletonResourceStore.convert_legacy_pools` or `preflight` call against a
 * saved resource, with the answer read back out of the stored document
 * afterwards; a refusal is recorded only after checking that the resource is
 * byte-for-byte what it was before the call. Document fixtures come from the
 * store's own committed tests, so an input here is a shape the store is
 * tested on rather than one this editor invented.
 *
 * Why this corpus exists. An inline entry has no Agent to reference, so the
 * conversion materialises one, and that is built on the Kernel configured on
 * the Agent node its Pool is attached to. But the entry may already DECLARE a
 * model, and then the runtime does not take the author's configuration as an
 * instruction — it compares the two and refuses `identity_mismatch` if they
 * differ, writing nothing. So the panel cannot offer the conversion without
 * first answering the runtime's question, and it cannot answer it by reading
 * `_model_matches` and writing something that looks like it. Two measured
 * reasons:
 *
 *   - the comparison is on `str.strip()`-ed text, and that is NOT `trim()`.
 *     A model name carrying U+001F, U+0085 or U+00A0 is stripped by the
 *     runtime and matches; one carrying U+FEFF is NOT stripped and does not.
 *     `trim()` has it exactly backwards on all four — see
 *     `mirror-the-rule-not-a-lookalike`, where the same two character sets
 *     cost a round on the endpoint rule.
 *   - the split is `split(":", 1)`, so `ollama:llama3:8b` is provider
 *     `ollama` and model `llama3:8b`, while the same name written bare —
 *     `llama3:8b` — is read as provider `llama3` and model `8b` and matches
 *     nothing. A tag is not a qualifier here.
 *
 * `accepted` is the store's own verdict and nothing here states what the
 * editor reads out of the entry: the mirror's answer is composed with
 * `profile_holds_model` in the test and compared against this column, so a
 * capture row cannot agree with the mirror by restating it.
 *
 * `template` and `document` are JSON TEXT, parsed at load: a legacy entry
 * reaches the editor by being parsed, and `legacy_fields` is the old node's
 * keys copied verbatim, so a JS literal cannot state every shape one can
 * hold. Each row of the owner table carries the WHOLE document the store was
 * given, so the mirror answers the same question about the same input rather
 * than about a shorter one written here. */

